{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01LQGKtoozMhuKQprTzsbDNG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#pragma once\n2\t#include \"data.h\"\n3\t\n4\t/**\n5\t * From the Grid, find all the pairs of particles that are colliding with each other, create a struct Contact for\n6\t * each one and save it into 'contacts'. Returns the number of collisions.\n7\t *\n8\t * The search for contacts is done as following:\n9\t *     1. Traverse each square in the grid. If it is empty (there is a null pointer in grid[square_idx]), then continue to the next square.\n10\t *          2. Iterate each particle inside the square. The particle iterated over will be 'p'.\n11\t *              3. If p is the only particle inside the square, continue to 5.\n12\t *                  4. Traverse all the square to find collisions with its  square-neighbors.\n13\t *              5. Traverse the particles that are inside squares that could be containing particles colliding with p. The squares can be found with the following method:\n14\t *                  a. Imagine a circle with twice the radius of p, called neighboring cicle. Every other coliding particle's center \n15\t *                     must be inside the neighboring circle, since all have the same radius. \n16\t *                  b. We have to find the set of squares such that part of its geometric area is overlaping with p's neighboring circle.\n17\t *                     We do not do this, but something similar. Instead of using a circle, we use the square in which the neighboring circle is inscribed.\n18\t *                  c. We find the rows and columns that contain the limits (upper, lower, left and right) of said square. These will define\n19\t *                     us the set of squares inside Grid that could contain p's colliding particles.\n20\t *                  d. Traverse all the particles inside each found square to find collisions.\n21\t */\n22\tsize_t compute_contacts(Particle const *const *const grid, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Contact* contacts);\n23\t\n24\t/**\n25\t * Fills the grid with particles. This updates the value of a pointer in grid when a particle is inside a square, and updates the pointer of the last\n26\t * particle in the last position of the singly linked list of the square. Running time is O(N), and memory space is O(N) but really 2*N because of 'grid_lasts' pointers array.\n27\t */\n28\tvoid fill_grid(const size_t num_particles, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Particle const *const particles, Particle* *const grid, Particle** grid_lasts);\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecaf5649-e0f5-4fc2-807d-ba5c6387f8e1","uuid":"788c32da-1a6b-4fb0-83d3-658b5a85f32f","timestamp":"2026-06-01T12:38:06.732Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-c90gnM/project/c_src/src/include/collisions.h","content":"#pragma once\n#include \"data.h\"\n\n/**\n * From the Grid, find all the pairs of particles that are colliding with each other, create a struct Contact for\n * each one and save it into 'contacts'. Returns the number of collisions.\n *\n * The search for contacts is done as following:\n *     1. Traverse each square in the grid. If it is empty (there is a null pointer in grid[square_idx]), then continue to the next square.\n *          2. Iterate each particle inside the square. The particle iterated over will be 'p'.\n *              3. If p is the only particle inside the square, continue to 5.\n *                  4. Traverse all the square to find collisions with its  square-neighbors.\n *              5. Traverse the particles that are inside squares that could be containing particles colliding with p. The squares can be found with the following method:\n *                  a. Imagine a circle with twice the radius of p, called neighboring cicle. Every other coliding particle's center \n *                     must be inside the neighboring circle, since all have the same radius. \n *                  b. We have to find the set of squares such that part of its geometric area is overlaping with p's neighboring circle.\n *                     We do not do this, but something similar. Instead of using a circle, we use the square in which the neighboring circle is inscribed.\n *                  c. We find the rows and columns that contain the limits (upper, lower, left and right) of said square. These will define\n *                     us the set of squares inside Grid that could contain p's colliding particles.\n *                  d. Traverse all the particles inside each found square to find collisions.\n */\nsize_t compute_contacts(Particle const *const *const grid, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Contact* contacts);\n\n/**\n * Fills the grid with particles. This updates the value of a pointer in grid when a particle is inside a square, and updates the pointer of the last\n * particle in the last position of the singly linked list of the square. Running time is O(N), and memory space is O(N) but really 2*N because of 'grid_lasts' pointers array.\n */\nvoid fill_grid(const size_t num_particles, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Particle const *const particles, Particle* *const grid, Particle** grid_lasts);\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}}}
